text:
{"annotator_prompt": [
{"kind": "modify", "field": "placed", "edits": [
{"modify_from": "The value is always written as a JSON array of double-quoted color strings; at step #1 it is the literal [] (an explicit empty array, never a blank).", "modify_to": "The value is the color words separated by single spaces, with no brackets, quotes or commas; at step #1 it is the word none."},
{"modify_from": "Example sequence over an episode: [] -> [] -> [\"green\"] -> [\"green\"] -> [\"green\", \"green\"]. e.g. [\"green\", \"green\"]", "modify_to": "Example sequence over an episode: none -> none -> green -> green -> green green. e.g. blue blue blue green"}
]},
{"kind": "add", "field": "arrivals", "description": "Ordered list of the [y, x] positions of the goal-color cubes currently lying on the table, in the order they first appeared in the episode, oldest first. Goal-color cubes are not all present at the start: new ones appear on the table while the robot works, and the oracle picks, within the color being worked, the one that has been on the table longest. At step #1 the list holds every goal-color cube in the scene, ordered by y as reported (smaller y first). At each later step: remove the entry of the cube the robot picked up during the step (the entry nearest where the fingers closed on a cube); append, at the end, every goal-color detection on the table in the scene at the decision step that matches no existing entry within about 12 px, except bin echoes (within about 28 px of the bin's reported center) and release echoes (a detection of the just-released color within about 12 px of where the held cube was last reported or of the gripper when the fingers opened); drop an entry that no longer has a detection of its color within about 12 px in the scene at the decision step; refresh each remaining entry to its position in the scene at the decision step. The annotator may use the whole episode to check which detections are real cubes (a real one is later picked or stays at the same place for the rest of the episode). e.g. [[70, 82], [95, 158], [79, 88]]"}
],
"policy_prompt_for_memory": [
{"kind": "modify", "field": "placed", "edits": [
{"modify_from": "Write the literal [] as this step's value only at the very first decision step, where the carried-in memory is blank and there are no execution steps at all. A carried-in [] that comes with execution steps is a list like any other and is appended to by the check below, so the step after the first placement writes a one-entry list such as [\"blue\"]. The value is always a JSON array of double-quoted strings, exactly like the carried-in value and the example: [\"green\", \"green\"], never ['green', 'green'] and never a blank.", "modify_to": "Write the word none as this step's value only at the very first decision step, where the carried-in memory is blank and there are no execution steps at all. The value is plain color words separated by single spaces and nothing else: no brackets, no quotes, no commas. A carried-in none that comes with execution steps is appended to by the check below like any other value: the first appended color replaces none, so the step after the first placement writes a single word such as blue."},
{"modify_from": "append that held cube's color exactly once.", "modify_to": "append one space and that held cube's color exactly once (if the carried-in value is none, replace it by the color)."},
{"modify_from": "e.g. carried-in [\"red\"] becomes [\"red\", \"red\"]; carried-in [\"blue\", \"blue\"] after a held-then-released blue cube becomes [\"blue\", \"blue\", \"blue\"].", "modify_to": "e.g. carried-in red becomes red red; carried-in blue blue after a held-then-released blue cube becomes blue blue blue."},
{"modify_from": "Emit the value character-for-character as a JSON array with double-quoted strings, identical in form to the carried-in value; a single-quoted list such as ['red', 'green'] is a wrong value. e.g. [\"green\", \"green\"]", "modify_to": "Emit the value as bare words only; any brackets, quotes or commas make it a wrong value. e.g. blue blue blue green"}
]},
{"kind": "add", "field": "arrivals", "description": "Ordered list of [y, x] positions of the goal-color cubes lying on the table, oldest-arrived first; the subgoal rule picks the first entry of the color it is working. At the very first decision step (carried-in memory blank, no execution frames) list every goal-color cube in the scene, ordered by y as reported (smaller y first). At every later step start from the carried-in list and apply, in order: (1) if an execution frame shows a cube held in the gripper, remove the entry nearest the gripper position in the frame where the fingers first closed on it (within about 12 px); (2) drop any entry that has no table detection of its color within about 12 px in the scene at this decision step (the last frame); (3) append, at the end, each goal-color table detection in the last frame that matches no remaining entry within about 12 px, unless it is a bin echo (within about 28 px of the bin's reported center) or a release echo (a detection of the just-released color within about 12 px of where the held cube was last reported or of the gripper at the frame the fingers opened); (4) update each remaining entry to its last-frame position. Order is never changed except by removal and appending. Write the list as nested brackets exactly like the example. e.g. [[70, 82], [95, 158], [79, 88]]"}
],
"policy_prompt_for_subgoal": [
{"modify_from": "using the scene shown and the memory field `placed` as updated at this step.", "modify_to": "using the scene shown and the memory fields `placed` and `arrivals` as updated at this step. `placed` is space-separated color words (the word none when nothing has been placed); a color's count is the number of times its word appears, and none counts as zero for every color."},
{"modify_from": "Color choice: a color whose count in the updated `placed` meets its requirement is finished and is never picked again, even if cubes of that color remain visible on the table. If the last entry of `placed` is a color that is still short, pick that color again (one color is finished before the other is started). Otherwise, including when `placed` is empty, take the first color in the goal instruction's order whose count in `placed` is still below its requirement.", "modify_to": "Color choice: a color whose count in the updated `placed` meets its requirement is finished and is never picked again, even if cubes of that color remain visible on the table. If the last word of `placed` is a color that is still short, pick that color again (one color is finished before the other is started). Otherwise, including when `placed` is none, the working color is the color of the first entry of `arrivals` whose color is still short (the oldest cube of a needed color on the table); the order in which the goal names the colors does not matter."},
{"modify_from": "A candidate is a cube of the needed color reported as a table cube (not held) in the scene at this decision step and at nearly the same place (within about 6 px) in all but at most one of the execution frames of this step; a real cube may be missing from one frame while the arm passes over it (at step #1, with no execution frames, any cube of that color in the scene is a candidate). A detection that first appears during the step, that sits where the held cube was last reported, that is reported within about 35 px of the bin's center (a detection about 30 px from the bin is a bin echo, not a cube), or whose position moves between frames is not a candidate while any stable one exists.", "modify_to": "A candidate is a cube of the working color reported as a table cube (not held) in the scene at this decision step. New cubes genuinely appear on the table during the episode, so a detection that was absent from earlier frames is still a candidate, and one that wobbles a few px between frames while the arm passes near it is still a candidate. Excluded, at every step including step #1: a detection within about 28 px of the bin's reported center (bin echo; a cube 30 px or more from the center that stays in place is real); a detection of the just-released color within about 12 px of where the held cube was last reported or of the gripper in the frame where the fingers opened (release echo), unless that same detection was already at the same place in the first execution frame of the step; and a detection whose position tracks the gripper from frame to frame."},
{"modify_from": "Among several stable candidates, prefer the one nearest the bin's reported center without overlapping it; when two candidates are within about 5 px of the same distance to the bin, prefer the one with the larger y (farther forward). The point is that candidate's center as reported in the last frame; a point within about 3 px of that center is correct.", "modify_to": "Among several candidates, choose the one that comes first in `arrivals` (match entries to last-frame detections within about 12 px): the oracle picks the cube of the working color that has been on the table longest. Only when the candidates cannot be told apart by `arrivals` (none of them matches an entry) fall back to the one nearest the bin's reported center that is not excluded above. The point is that candidate's center as reported in the last frame; a point within about 3 px of that center is correct."},
{"modify_from": "An episode starts in A at step #1: the gripper is raised out of the frame, `placed` is empty, so pick up a cube of a needed color.", "modify_to": "An episode starts in A at step #1: the gripper is raised out of the frame, `placed` is none, so pick up the cube that is first in `arrivals` among those of a needed color."},
{"modify_from": "otherwise choose A pointing at the stable cube of a needed color nearest the bin (not overlapping it), and if none is visible at all, choose A pointing at any visible cube not overlapping the bin.", "modify_to": "otherwise choose A pointing at the first `arrivals` entry of a needed color, else the cube of a needed color nearest the bin (not within 28 px of it), and if none is visible at all, choose A pointing at any visible cube not within 28 px of the bin."}
],
"refused": "The report's rule 'work the color with the largest required count first, tie by goal order' is not committed. Round 1 recorded placed=[\"green\"] under a goal of three blue and two green, where green was started although blue needed more, and round 2 recorded every episode starting with the first-named color; round 3 now records the second-named and the larger-count color first. Each count- or order-based rule fits one round and contradicts another, so instead the starting color is taken from the new `arrivals` field: the color of the oldest goal-color cube on the table. This is consistent with round 3's own finding that most episodes open with a single cube and others appear later, and it does not contradict the rounds 1 and 2 picks, which it covers as arbitrary-looking choices driven by which cube was present first. The finish-one-color-before-the-other rule from round 1 stays. The one-step tie-break proposals (smaller x, farthest from bin, nearest gripper, smaller y, nearest bin after a 40 px discard) stay out, as in rounds 1 and 2; nearest-bin survives only as a fallback when `arrivals` cannot separate the candidates. The proposal that any fixed-position detection is a real cube regardless of bin distance stays out; the bin-echo radius is set to 28 px, which keeps round 2's observed 30 px echo case covered through the release-echo exclusion rather than by radius alone. The stability test is removed as the report asks, but a clause excluding a detection that tracks the gripper is kept, because round 1 recorded that failure and the new filter would otherwise reopen it. The `placed` form change to bare words is committed because the quoting slip has survived three rounds of instruction text and the form change is the only edit that removes it."}
